From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: mzak <mzak.amin@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 2 Oct 2026 19:25:24 +0300
Subject: [PATCH] hwmon: add a driver for the Qualcomm PMIC power telemetry

The PMK8850 accumulates the output power of the switching regulators
that supply the CPU clusters, the GPU, the NSP and the SoC's CX / MX
rails in its SDAM blocks (QPT, Qualcomm power telemetry). Each sample
period an accumulator grows by its regulator's power in units of a
scale that the boot firmware configures, so one count is scale x
sample period of energy. The totals reach the data SDAM once per
report (about a second) with a time stamp in milliseconds of the
nominal 32 kHz timer, while the samples are taken from the 32.768 kHz
sleep clock.

Add a hwmon driver for it:

- the configuration (regulators, sample timer, scale) is read from the
  configuration SDAM; a regulator is measured when both of its enable
  bits are set
- the client costs nothing while it is stopped, and the vendor's user
  space keeps it stopped until a client asks; so the driver never starts
  it at probe: the first read of an energy or power attribute starts it
  with the vendor driver's values (0x80 into the data SDAM's control
  byte, then the PBS trigger), and it is stopped again idle_ms after the
  last such read (module parameter, default 10 s), at every system
  suspend and when the driver goes; while stopped, the accumulators keep
  their values and no power window spans the stop (the time stamp jumps
  by the stopped time at the first report)
- the accumulators are read on demand, with a re-read when the report
  count moved during the read and a plausibility limit against reads
  that catch the client mid-write; restarts of the accumulators are
  followed, so the energy counters stay monotonic
- per regulator: energyN_input (since probe, with the sleep clock's
  sample period) and powerN_average over the reports of a window of
  update_interval ms (count rise x scale / samples, the samples from
  the report time stamps, as the vendor driver computes its average),
  with the label from the device tree or "s<k><pmic>" for SMPS k of a
  PMIC
- the overflow interrupt is enabled only while the client runs and is
  handled as the vendor driver does (read the counts, acknowledge
  through the fraction SDAM's PBS trigger); it is no wakeup source; the
  per-report interrupt is not used, so the telemetry never wakes the
  CPUs
- debugfs qcom_qpt/raw for bring-up: configuration bytes, the decoded
  timer and scale, the control and time stamp bytes, the raw counts

Register layout and coding follow the vendor's qti_power_telemetry
driver. Checked on the OnePlus Pad 4: the report time stamps advance
1022 ms per second of the kernel clock (1024 for 32.768 kHz, 1000 for
the vendor's 32 kHz), and the fraction SDAM's live per-phase counts sum
to twice the data counts, the ratio of the 6400 uW unit (spare byte
set) to CONFIG0's 3200 uW.

Signed-off-by: mzak <mzak.amin@gmail.com>
Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
